# Studio authentication vs authorization

**Never assume a signed-in user is allowed to act.** Entra + SWA Easy Auth only prove identity. Authorization is application-level and must be re-checked on every privileged call.

## Authentication (open)

- Any user who can complete Entra login for this tenant (members and guests) must be able to sign in.
- Keep `require_app_role_assignment` **false** (`azuread_service_principal.swa`). Setting it true causes **AADSTS50105** and blocks login before the app can authorize.
- Do **not** use Entra “Assignment required?”, Users and groups assignments, or SWA `authenticated` as the publish/CRM permission model.
- SWA still requires SWA role **`studio`** for `/studio`, `/studio/*`, and `authenticated` for `/api/*` (except the documented public exceptions). That is a login + role gate, not an action grant.
- Apps stay `AzureADMyOrg` with the tenant-specific `openIdIssuer`. Do not switch to `/common/v2.0`.

## Authorization (one catalog)

Discrete permissions live in [`api/src/lib/permissions.js`](api/src/lib/permissions.js). Roles are named bundles. User profiles (`studioUsers` table) are the source of truth. `ALLOWED_USER_IDS` only bootstraps a missing **Super Administrator** profile on first session — it is not a second publish gate. Architecture: [`docs/architecture/authentication-authorization.md`](docs/architecture/authentication-authorization.md). Do not name that role Owner (Azure RBAC / Entra app owner are different).

| Action | Check on every call |
|--------|---------------------|
| Publish / upload / discrete / publish status | `permissionGate(..., 'content.publish')` (also `publisherGate()`) |
| People / CRM | `permissionGate(..., 'people.read' \| 'people.write')` — one CRM per deployment (`STUDIO_CONTACTS_PARTITION`); never a tenant id from the body |
| Access admin | `permissionGate(..., 'users.read' \| 'users.manage')` |
| Calendar connect | `permissionGate(..., 'calendar.connect')` |
| Lesson schedule | `permissionGate(..., 'calendar.read' \| 'calendar.write')` |
| Public `contactInquiry` | Turnstile + schema; no Studio principal |
| Public `lessonPayConfig` | Flag + sanitized Payment Link URLs only |
| Public `contactAccountConfig` | Flag `{ enabled }` only |
| Public `authRoles` | SWA rolesSource assignment (`studio` \| `contact`) |
| Public `stripeWebhook` | Stripe signature verification |
| Public `calendarWatch` | Google channel token |
| Public `lessonAction` | Signed Confirm / Decline token (ICS fallback) |

Azure Functions use `authLevel: 'anonymous'` because SWA presents the principal. That is **not** a skip — handlers must still call `permissionGate`. Local `AZURE_FUNCTIONS_ENVIRONMENT=Development` grants the full catalog (`func start` without SWA headers).

Hub UI reads `GET /api/studioSession` (same payload as `publisherStatus`) and shows tiles from `permissions[]`. That is convenience only. The API remains the enforcement point.

## When adding a Studio API

1. Add a discrete permission ID to the catalog (and a role bundle if needed).
2. Call `permissionGate(request, PERMISSION.…)` **before** reading secrets, calling Gemini, writing git, or touching Table Storage.
3. Return a friendly error + `correlationId`. Do not leak whether the allowlist or profile table is empty.
4. Gate matching Studio UI from the session permissions in the same PR.
5. Update [`docs/runbooks/manage-access.md`](docs/runbooks/manage-access.md) if the layers change.
6. If the catalog, roles, bootstrap, or login gates change, update [`docs/architecture/authentication-authorization.md`](docs/architecture/authentication-authorization.md).
7. If the API persists or stops persisting records, update [`docs/architecture/data-persistence.md`](docs/architecture/data-persistence.md) — see [data-persistence.mdc](data-persistence.mdc).

See [`api/src/lib/studioAccess.js`](api/src/lib/studioAccess.js) and [`docs/runbooks/manage-access.md`](docs/runbooks/manage-access.md).
